{document
title: "Filling named placeholders in Lateralus"
}

{h2 Fill a message from a map}

Keep the message in a single string and pass its values in a map.
The template module replaces each `<<key>>` with the value stored under
that key.

{code lang="lateralus"
let subject = "Hello <<name>>, your order ships <<day>>."
let fields = {}
fields["name"] = "Sam"
fields["day"] = "Friday"
println(render(subject, fields))
}

This prints `Hello Sam, your order ships Friday.`

{h2 Choose what happens when a value is missing}

If the map contains only the name, `render` leaves `<<day>>` in the
output. That makes a missing field visible when checking a draft. For
an optional field in a final message, use `render_default`. Its third
argument supplies the same fallback for every missing key.

{code lang="lateralus"
let fields_without_day = {}
fields_without_day["name"] = "Sam"
println(render_default(subject, fields_without_day, "soon"))
}

This prints `Hello Sam, your order ships soon.`

{h2 Run the example}

From the repository root on macOS or Linux, feed the string helpers,
the template module, and the example to the compiler as one source:

{code lang="sh"
cat stdlib/strings.ltl stdlib/template.ltl examples/template_tutorial.ltl |
  lateralus run /dev/stdin
}

The Python runner does not load `stdlib.template` through a dotted
import, so the command supplies the actual module source explicitly.
The example checks the exact output before printing each line:

{code lang="text"
Hello Sam, your order ships Friday.
Hello Sam, your order ships <<day>>.
Hello Sam, your order ships soon.
}
  </script>
  <script src="../ltlml.js"></script>
